  "worked_solution": "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=2-\\frac{6}{x+1}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{6}{x+1} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{x+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{6}{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 6 & =2(x+1) \\\\ 6 & =2x+2 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=1$ and $x=5$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [1,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
